- op: create  # c57f
  at: '2026-08-30T05:59:14Z'  # c57f
  by: agent  # c57f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c57f
  clock: 1  # c57f
  nonce: c57f  # c57f
  data:  # c57f
    kind: invariant  # c57f
    title: "A handle-constructor row's boundary is a property of its ARGUMENT: bufio.NewScanner is declared ipc_recv unconditionally and in the 0010 corpus wraps a file or an in-memory buffer every single time"  # c57f
    status: violated  # c57f
    priority: 2  # c57f
    parent:  # c57f
    tags:  # c57f
    - catalogue  # c57f
    - io_boundary  # c57f
    - analysis_quality  # c57f
    isbefore: []  # c57f
    duplicate_of: []  # c57f
    not_duplicate_of: []  # c57f
    pr_ref:  # c57f
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-30 by measurement 0010, which adjudicated 17 situations under the\nmechanism it named WRONG-CHANNEL.\n\nTHE OBSERVATION. `bufio.NewScanner` and `bufio.NewReader` are declared\n`ipc_recv` in go.yaml, unconditionally. They wrap an `io.Reader`, and WHICH\nBOUNDARY THE CROSSING IS depends entirely on what that argument is:\n\n  bufio.NewScanner(os.Stdin)                     ipc_recv     -- correct\n  bufio.NewScanner(f)               f = os.Open  fs_read      -- WRONG\n  bufio.NewScanner(bytes.NewReader(output))      no crossing  -- WRONG\n                                    output = cmd.Output()     (the real one is\n                                                               the pipe read)\n  bufio.NewScanner(strings.NewReader(string(output)))         -- WRONG\n\nMEASURED, in the 0010 cohort. EVERY `bufio.NewScanner` site adjudicated wraps a\nfile or an in-memory buffer; NOT ONE wraps stdin:\n\n  beads   delete.go:305, doctor/jsonl_integrity.go:55,\n          doctor/fix/migrate_tombstones.go:95, sync_check.go:237,\n          sync_import.go:52, doctor/integrity.go:370          -- a FILE\n  beads   import.go:664, init.go:940, jira.go:421/433/560,\n          status.go:180/218                                    -- a git command's stdout\n  cilium  iptables.go:213, iptables.go:1038                    -- iptables-save stdout\n  cilium  loader/compile.go:224                                -- a compiler's stderr\n\nTEN of arm 2's twenty-four true positives are vacuous (KIND-MISDECLARED) for\nthis reason and not for ADR-0049's. `bufio.NewReader` has the same shape and\ndoes sometimes wrap stdin, which is what makes it harder rather than easier:\none row cannot be right for both.\n\nTHIS IS INV-vaduk'S PROBLEM IN A NEW PLACE. That item ruled on rows that carry\nTWO boundaries because the catalogue cannot express why. Here the row carries\nONE boundary that is only sometimes true, and which one is true is a property of\nthe ARGUMENT AT THE CALL SITE -- something the catalogue has no vocabulary for\nat all. It is also INV-nular's rule (\"kind asserted by name, never checked\nagainst semantics\") reaching a case where checking the name cannot help.\n\nNOT THE SAME AS THE SIBLING ITEM. That one says the transfer calls are absent\nfrom the catalogue, which BLOCKS a retag. This one says the constructor rows\nare on the wrong boundary a majority of the time, which is a precision defect\nthat exists whether or not anything is ever retagged.\n\nTHREE CANDIDATE SHAPES, none chosen here:\n  (a) argument-conditioned rows -- new catalogue vocabulary, largest change;\n  (b) drop the constructor rows once the READ calls are catalogued (the sibling\n      item), which resolves this by making the constructors unnecessary;\n  (c) a weaker boundary for wrapper constructors that mints nothing and\n      discloses, which is ADR-0049's net_listen shape applied to ipc.\n\n(b) is the cheapest and is already sequenced by the sibling item. Recording all\nthree so the choice is visible rather than defaulted.\n\nREPRO / EVIDENCE\n  docs/measurements/0010-deferred-crossing-findings-per-shape.md § mechanism table\n  ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/step3_adjudication_08302026/arm2_ledger.json"  # c57f
    fields:  # c57f
      statement: "A catalogue row's io_boundary must be true of every call site the row matches."  # c57f
      root_cause: "bufio.NewScanner/NewReader wrap an arbitrary io.Reader, so which boundary the crossing is depends on the argument; one row cannot express that, and the catalogue has no argument-conditioned vocabulary."  # c57f
- op: discuss  # 5c6b
  at: '2026-08-30T08:40:58Z'  # 5c6b
  by: agent  # 5c6b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5c6b
  clock: 2  # 5c6b
  nonce: 5c6b  # 5c6b
  message: "THE THREE-OPTION MENU IS ANSWERED BY A RULING THAT ALREADY EXISTS, and it\nrejects all three. INV-zumin (satisfied, P1) partitioned exactly this population\n-- primitives declared under more than one boundary -- into (a) disambiguated at\nmatch time by an argument, (b) UNDECIDABLE AT THE CALL SITE, (c) simultaneously\ntrue. bufio.NewScanner / NewReader are textbook (b): C's unistd.read is\ncatalogued fs_read + net_recv + ipc_recv with the written reason \"the fd's kind\nis not knowable at the call site\", which is this row's problem verbatim.\n\nINV-zumin's closure says two things that settle the menu:\n  * \"needs fd-type inference, not row reordering\" -- so (a), argument-conditioned\n    ROWS, is not the shape; the inference is.\n  * \"Multiplying the (b) rows would be actively wrong: a net_send chain for every\n    C write to stdout is a FALSE VIOLATION, the expensive direction.\" Its\n    `simultaneous: true` marker was deliberately scoped to (c) and deliberately\n    withheld from (b) -- so (c), the disclosure/weaker-boundary shape, is\n    withheld here on the same reasoning.\nAnd (b), \"drop the constructor rows once the READ calls are catalogued\", is\nrefuted by measurement rather than by ruling: the read's boundary is the SAME\nproperty of the SAME argument one hop later, so cataloguing bufio.Reader.\nReadString at a fixed boundary relocates this defect instead of resolving it.\nSee WI-vutav for the fixture run.\n\nTHE CENSUS, which is larger and worse than the adjudicated sample this item was\nfiled from. Every bufio.NewScanner / bufio.NewReader call site in the cohort's\nGo repositories, bucketed by first argument\n(~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/vutav_reads_08302026/wraps.py, n=236):\n\n    153  (64.8%)  bare local var -- UNDECIDABLE from the call site alone\n     45  (19.1%)  in-memory buffer (strings/bytes.NewReader) -- NO CROSSING\n     15  ( 6.4%)  other expression\n     11  ( 4.7%)  os.Stdin                        <- the boundary the row asserts\n     11  ( 4.7%)  net.Conn                        -> net_recv\n      1  ( 0.4%)  http response body              -> net_recv\n  per repo: cilium 160, beads 69, caddy 5, jaeger 2\n\nSo the declared ipc_recv is demonstrably right for 4.7% of sites and asserts a\ncrossing that does not exist for at least 19.1%. The item's statement -- \"a\ncatalogue row's io_boundary must be true of every call site the row matches\" --\nis violated at census scale, not just on the 17 adjudicated situations.\n\nAND THE INFERENCE INV-zumin ASKS FOR IS NOW HALF BUILT. 64.8% of sites need the\norigin of a local variable, which is a dataflow question, not a syntactic one --\nso an argument-conditioned ROW could not have answered them even if the\nvocabulary existed. What the constructor's argument DOES have, at the call site,\nis an edge: `sc := bufio.NewScanner(f)` where `f, _ := os.Open(p)` has `os.Open`\nalready classified fs_read. The boundary is recoverable by propagating from the\nargument's own classification. WI-vutav's fix supplies the missing half of that\n-- the handle's type now resolves -- so the remaining work is the propagation.\nFiled separately.\n\nONE CORRECTION TO THIS ITEM'S TEXT: \"TEN of arm 2's twenty-four true positives\nare vacuous (KIND-MISDECLARED)\" is a pass-1 fossil. Published 0010 reports 7 TP\ntotal and KIND-MISDECLARED = 0; under the corrected pass these 17 WRONG-CHANNEL\nsituations are FALSE POSITIVES, not vacuous true ones. That makes the item\nstronger, not weaker -- pure false-positive volume."  # 5c6b
- op: discuss  # d965
  at: '2026-09-02T07:14:05Z'  # d965
  by: agent  # d965
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d965
  clock: 3  # d965
  nonce: d965  # d965
  message: "RE-DERIVED AND RE-DIAGNOSED 2026-09-02 on dev 5a7aa6ce6a. THE ITEM REPRODUCES,\nBUT ITS THREE-OPTION MENU AND THE PRIOR ENTRY'S \"ALL THREE ARE REJECTED\"\nVERDICT ARE BOTH OVERTAKEN: the inference INV-zumin demanded EXISTS AND IS\nCORRECT ALREADY, and the fix is one catalogue row. What blocks it is something\nneither this item nor that entry names.\n\nREPRODUCED, five argument shapes, each cold in its own XDG_CACHE_HOME:\n\n    bufio.NewScanner(os.Stdin)                    -> ipc_recv   CORRECT\n    bufio.NewScanner(f), f, _ := os.Open(p)       -> ipc_recv   WRONG (fs_read)\n    bufio.NewScanner(bytes.NewReader(b))          -> no chain   CORRECT\n    bufio.NewScanner(bytes.NewReader(cmdOutput))  -> no chain\n    bufio.NewScanner(c), c a net.Conn parameter   -> ipc_recv   WRONG (net_recv)\n\n*** THE ARGUMENT-ORIGIN INFERENCE IS ALREADY BUILT AND ALREADY RIGHT. *** The\nprior entry says INV-zumin's closure \"needs fd-type inference, not row\nreordering\", and treats that as the blocker. WI-lipis shipped the inference\nsince. Reading the edge meta directly:\n\n    os.Stdin                     -> io_target_kind = std_stream\n    f, _ := os.Open(p)           -> io_target_kind = host_path   (multi-value\n                                    assignment IS handled)\n    bytes.NewReader(b)           -> io_target_kind = in_memory\n    net.Conn parameter           -> io_target_kind ABSENT (correctly abstains)\n\nSo the stamp is produced, correct, and fails closed. `read_boundary_for_target\n_kind` already maps host_path -> fs_read and std_stream -> ipc_recv, and the\nin_memory case ALREADY works end to end (that probe emits no chain at all).\n\n*** WHY THE host_path CASE STILL CLASSIFIES ipc_recv IS ONE SENTENCE IN THE\nCODE. *** `_target_kind_discriminated_keys` requires TWO OR MORE READ\nBOUNDARIES before a stamped kind may choose between them, and it says so about\nthis exact primitive: \"bufio.NewScanner is ipc_recv alone. One row is not a\nchoice, so go's behaviour after WI-lipis's second deliverable is unchanged and\nits target kinds keep reaching only the mint gate.\" go.yaml declares\nNewScanner/NewReader under ipc_recv ONLY, on the note \"When wrapping os.Stdin\".\nThe catalogue never gave the seam anything to narrow.\n\nTHE FIX IS THEREFORE THE SHIPPED c.yaml/haskell.yaml SHAPE: declare the row\nunder fs_read AND ipc_recv with boundary_ruling: call_site_undecidable, and let\nthe existing seam pick. I built it and measured it.\n\nA/B, six Go repositories, both arms cold, baseline asserted by grepping the\nruntime file:\n\n    repo          base   fix   delta   bufio moves\n    alertmanager   267   267      +0   ipc_recv->fs_read 3\n    beads         2676  2676      +0   ipc_recv->fs_read 41\n    caddy          298   298      +0   ipc_recv->fs_read 4\n    gocryptfs      389   389      +0   -\n    jaeger         446   446      +0   ipc_recv->fs_read 2\n    prometheus     882   882      +0   ipc_recv->fs_read 1\n    TOTAL: 51 rows change boundary, 0 chains added, 0 lost.\n\n*** AND IT CANNOT SHIP, FOR A REASON WORTH MORE THAN THE FIX. *** 46 of the 51\nare correct -- beads' 41 are all JSONL/file readers, plus lexer.load,\nparseEnvFile, readPrometheusLabels. FIVE ARE WRONG, and they are wrong in the\ndirection that matters:\n\n    alertmanager cluster/tls_connection.go:136  bufio.NewReader(conn.connection)\n        -- a TLS network connection\n    caddy httpredirectlistener.go:90            bufio.NewReader(c)\n        -- c is a net.Conn from l.Listener.Accept()\n    plus alertmanager nflog.go:292 / silence.go:1319\n\nAll read back against source. All have io_target_kind = None: they did not move\nbecause they were stamped, they moved because THE ABSTENTION DEFAULT FLIPPED.\nAnd fs_read mints no taint source, so each one LOSES A TRUE untrusted_input\nSOURCE. Trading 46 false-positive removals for 5 false-NEGATIVE introductions\nis the false-all-clear direction.\n\nThe fix that keeps all 46 and loses none is obvious -- make ipc_recv the\nabstention default for THIS primitive, so only positively-stamped sites move.\nIT IS NOT AVAILABLE.\n\n*** THE ABSTENTION DEFAULT IS A GLOBAL REGISTRY ORDER, NOT A PER-CATALOGUE OR\nPER-ROW PROPERTY. *** `IoBoundaryCatalog._from_dict` iterates\n`for boundary in CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES`, so a primitive declared under two\nboundaries always resolves, on abstention, to whichever comes first in that\nfrozen list -- fs_read is index 0, ipc_recv index 4 -- IN EVERY LANGUAGE.\nDocument order in the YAML is irrelevant: I moved go.yaml's whole fs_read block\nbelow ipc_recv, verified the parsed content byte-identical, and the primitive\nindices did not move (68/69 fs_read, 155/156 ipc_recv).\n\nc.yaml's note \"THIS ROW STAYS FIRST\" therefore describes the REGISTRY order,\nnot its own document order -- the two happen to agree there, which is why this\nhas never surfaced. And c/haskell WANT fs_read first: they started fs_read-only\n(a false NEGATIVE, no source minted) so their conservative direction is toward\nNOT minting. Go started ipc_recv-only (a false POSITIVE) so its conservative\ndirection is the opposite one. Same mechanism, mirrored default, and the\nmechanism can only express one.\n\nTHREE WAYS FORWARD, none chosen here, and the cheapest is not obviously right:\n  (a) a per-row abstention declaration (`abstains_to: ipc_recv`, or letting\n      boundary_ruling carry it). Smallest, and it makes explicit a fact that is\n      currently implicit in a registry list nobody reads as a precedence order.\n  (b) a target kind that names a network stream, so the net.Conn sites STAMP\n      instead of abstaining. Fixes caddy's `c` (bound from Accept) but NOT\n      alertmanager's `conn.connection`, which is a struct FIELD and\n      unresolvable -- the WI-dizag / INV-linub L3 family again.\n  (c) ship it and accept 5 lost sources for 46 corrected channels. Defensible\n      on volume, and the wrong call for a security tool.\n\nRECOMMEND (a). It is a small, local change; it turns an implicit global\nprecedence into a declared per-row one; and it is the only option that keeps\nall 46 wins with zero losses. NOT BUILT HERE because changing how catalogue\nprecedence is expressed touches every language's rows, and this item is scoped\nto one primitive -- filing the mechanism separately is the project's own rule.\n\nSTATUS STAYS violated. Nothing shipped; go.yaml is untouched at dev."  # d965
- op: discuss  # 14f4
  at: '2026-09-02T08:28:29Z'  # 14f4
  by: agent  # 14f4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 14f4
  clock: 4  # 14f4
  nonce: 14f4  # 14f4
  message: "THE FILE HALF IS FIXED in PR merged as dev b730e10d41, riding INV-fatok's\n`abstains_to`. STATUS STAYS violated, RE-SCOPED TO THE NETWORK CASE, and the\nreason is this item's own statement: \"a catalogue row's io_boundary must be\ntrue of EVERY call site the row matches\". It now is, wherever the argument's\norigin resolves. It still is not where the stream is a network connection.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. bufio.NewScanner/NewReader are declared under fs_read AND\nipc_recv with boundary_ruling: call_site_undecidable and abstains_to: ipc_recv,\nso the existing `_narrow_by_target_kind` seam picks using the io_target_kind\nthe analyzer has been stamping correctly since WI-lipis. The blocker was never\nthe inference INV-zumin asked for -- that has existed for weeks -- it was that\n`_target_kind_discriminated_keys` requires TWO OR MORE read boundaries before a\nstamp may choose, and this primitive had one row. The code said so about this\nexact primitive: \"bufio.NewScanner is ipc_recv alone. One row is not a choice.\"\n\nEVIDENCE, merged tree, each case cold in its own XDG_CACHE_HOME:\n\n    bufio.NewScanner(os.Stdin)                  -> ipc_recv   correct\n    bufio.NewScanner(f), f, _ := os.Open(p)     -> fs_read    FIXED\n    bufio.NewScanner(bytes.NewReader(b))        -> no chain   correct\n    bufio.NewScanner(c), c a net.Conn parameter -> ipc_recv   STILL WRONG\n                                                   (should be net_recv)\n\nCORPUS A/B, six Go repositories, both arms cold, baseline asserted by grepping\nthe runtime files: 42 rows move ipc_recv -> fs_read, 0 chains added, 0 lost.\nEvery move rests on a positive host_path stamp. Read back against source:\njaeger cmd/anonymizer/app/uiconv/reader.go:31 opens with os.OpenFile(...,\nO_RDONLY) and hands the handle to bufio.NewReader; beads' forty are JSONL file\nreaders.\n\n*** THE THREE-OPTION MENU AND ITS \"ALL THREE REJECTED\" ANSWER ARE BOTH\nSUPERSEDED, AND SO IS MY OWN FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS. *** The prior entry read\nINV-zumin as forbidding (a) argument-conditioned rows, (b) dropping the\nconstructors, and (c) a weaker disclosure boundary. What shipped is none of\nthose: it is the SHIPPED c.yaml/haskell.yaml shape -- two rows plus a\ncall-site-undecidable ruling -- consuming an inference that did not exist when\nINV-zumin ruled. A ruling against a catalogue ROW was not a ruling against the\nMECHANISM.\n\nAnd my first version of this fix was WRONG in the expensive direction. Adding\nthe second row alone moves 51 rows, five of them network reads that merely\nABSTAINED (alertmanager cluster/tls_connection.go:136 wraps a TLS connection,\ncaddy httpredirectlistener.go:90 wraps a net.Conn returned by Accept, both\nio_target_kind absent), and fs_read mints no taint source, so each lost a TRUE\nuntrusted_input source. That is what INV-fatok was filed for and it is why this\ndid not ship until the fallback was declarable. Nine rows stopped moving in\ntotal once it was -- four more were file-shaped rows also riding the default\nrather than a stamp -- so the honest figure is 42 evidence-backed retags, not\nthe 46-plus-5-mistakes the first run suggested.\n\nWHAT REMAINS, and it is a smaller and better-defined item than what was filed.\nA net.Conn argument is a REAL network receive and still classifies ipc_recv. It\ncannot be fixed by a row: there is no `io_target_kind` value naming a network\nstream, so a net_recv row could never be SELECTED and adding one would be an\nunreachable declaration making the catalogue look more capable than it is. Two\nsteps, in order:\n\n  1. a target kind for a network stream, so `bufio.NewScanner(c)` where c comes\n     from a resolvable net.* origin stamps instead of abstaining;\n  2. receiver typing for the UNRESOLVABLE origins -- alertmanager's\n     `conn.connection` is a struct FIELD, which is the WI-dizag / INV-linub L3\n     family, and no target-kind vocabulary reaches it.\n\nStep 1 alone fixes caddy's site (c is a local bound from Accept) and not\nalertmanager's. Neither is attempted here.\n\nCENSUS FIGURE THIS ITEM CARRIES, unchanged and still the right frame: of 236\nbufio sites, 4.7% wrap os.Stdin, 19.1% an in-memory buffer, 4.7% a net.Conn,\nand 64.8% a bare local. The bare locals are what WI-lipis's origin reading\nturned from undecidable into decided, and they are the 42."  # 14f4
- op: update  # aeca
  at: '2026-09-02T08:28:41Z'  # aeca
  by: agent  # aeca
  actor: jgstern_agent  # aeca
  clock: 5  # aeca
  nonce: aeca  # aeca
  set:  # aeca
    fields:  # aeca
      statement: "A catalogue row's io_boundary must be true of every call site the row matches. VIOLATED, NETWORK CASE ONLY as of 2026-09-02: bufio.NewScanner/NewReader now follow their argument's origin where it resolves (fs_read over an os.Open handle, ipc_recv over os.Stdin, no crossing over an in-memory buffer), but a net.Conn argument is a real network receive and still classifies ipc_recv, because no io_target_kind value names a network stream and a net_recv row could therefore never be selected."  # aeca
      root_cause: "The io_target_kind vocabulary has no value for a network stream, so the narrowing seam cannot select a net_recv row and one would be an unreachable declaration. Downstream of that, an unresolvable origin (a struct field such as alertmanager's conn.connection) abstains whatever the vocabulary contains, which is the WI-dizag / INV-linub L3 receiver-typing family."  # aeca
- op: discuss  # b1f3
  at: '2026-09-03T09:05:27Z'  # b1f3
  by: agent  # b1f3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b1f3
  clock: 6  # b1f3
  nonce: b1f3  # b1f3
  message: "THE MISSING VALUE THIS ITEM'S NETWORK CASE WAITS ON IS NOW SCHEDULED: WI-suhug (P1, 2026-09-03) introduces `net_stream` into the io_target_kind vocabulary for the write side (io.WriteString(conn, ...) / fmt.Fprintf(w http.ResponseWriter, ...) -> net_send) and, because the vocabulary gate moves both sides together, on the read side net_stream -> net_recv -- which is exactly what would let a net_recv row for bufio.NewScanner(conn) be SELECTED rather than be the unreachable declaration this item's note refuses. The go classifier learns the value from the receiver's TYPE (net.Conn, *tls.Conn, http.ResponseWriter) via var_types, not from a producer call. Re-measure this item's five network sites (alertmanager tls_connection.go:136, caddy httpredirectlistener.go:90 and peers) once WI-suhug lands."  # b1f3
